"""Original practice based on the supplied 96-page Lecture 05 PDF."""
GROUP={'name':'Lecture 05','title':'Prototyping & walkthroughs','description':'Conceptual models, prototypes, visual design, heuristics, cognitive walkthroughs and predictive models.','kind':'lecture'}
def build_prototyping_questions():
    bank=[]
    def m(q,a,wrong,why,pages,topic,level='application'):
        i=1+sum(x['type']=='mcq' for x in bank)
        bank.append(dict(id=f'l5-m{i:02}',group='l5',type='mcq',prompt=q,options=[a]+wrong,correct=0,explanation=why,source=f'Lecture 05 · PDF {pages}',topic=topic,origin='lecture',level=level))
    def s(q,a,b,pages,topic,points):
        i=1+sum(x['type']=='short' for x in bank)
        bank.append(dict(id=f'l5-s{i:02}',group='l5',type='short',prompt=q,answer=[a,b],keyPoints=points,source=f'Lecture 05 · PDF {pages}',topic=topic,origin='lecture',level='application'))
    m('What does a conceptual model primarily describe?', 'The system’s high-level concepts, organization and relationships', ['Only its final colours and fonts','Its complete source code and database implementation','The number of participants in a usability experiment'], 'The conceptual model explains how the system is organized and operates, without requiring implementation detail.','pp. 3–5','Conceptual models','concept')
    m('Why challenge the claim that everyone will happily wear special glasses for a new TV?', 'It is an assumption about users that needs evidence', ['Technological novelty guarantees acceptance','Users must adapt to every design choice','A conceptual model removes the need for research'], 'Conceptualization should expose assumptions and prevent baseless claims about user acceptance.','pp. 3–4','Design assumptions')
    m('A shopping cart helps users understand collecting items before purchase. What is it doing?', 'Using a familiar metaphor to explain an unfamiliar interaction', ['Proving that all physical cart properties must be copied','Measuring pointing time','Replacing every need for feedback'], 'Metaphors transfer useful familiar knowledge, but software need not copy all properties of the physical example.','pp. 5–7, 58','Metaphors')
    m('Which example best fits the lecture’s exploring interaction type?', 'Moving through a virtual museum to discover exhibits', ['Typing a command to delete a file','Filling a fixed postal-address form','Calculating the mean of task times'], 'Exploring involves moving through a virtual environment or physical space.','p. 8','Interaction types')
    m('Which set best characterizes direct manipulation?', 'Visible objects, physical actions and rapid reversible changes with immediate feedback', ['Hidden commands, complex syntax and delayed results','A compulsory natural-language conversation','A static image with no representation of objects'], 'The lecture lists continuous representation, physical actions and rapid reversible actions with immediate feedback.','p. 11','Interaction styles')
    m('Why can menus and forms be easier to learn than an unfamiliar command language?', 'They present choices for recognition instead of requiring exact syntax recall', ['They remove all possible errors','They always outperform commands for every expert','They never require labels'], 'Visible choices can reduce recall demands; command languages may remain useful for expert work.','pp. 10–12','Interaction styles')
    m('What is a discoverability risk of speech dialog?', 'Users may not know what commands or requests the system supports', ['All spoken requests are necessarily understood','Speech always displays a complete menu','Natural language guarantees a correct mental model'], 'Speech can be approachable yet leave capabilities and valid requests unclear.','p. 13','Interaction styles')
    m('Why build a rough prototype early?', 'Explore alternatives and obtain reactions before investing heavily in implementation', ['Prove that the final system is complete','Avoid involving users','Guarantee accurate production response times'], 'Cheap, changeable representations make alternatives easier to evaluate and discard.','pp. 21–25','Prototyping purpose')
    m('Which claim is least justified by testing a paper sketch?', 'The finished system will respond within 100 milliseconds', ['Users can comment on the proposed grouping','The team can compare alternative conceptual arrangements','Participants can suggest a clearer action label'], 'A paper representation does not reproduce the implemented system’s response time or interaction feel.','pp. 25, 28','Fidelity limits')
    m('What do a series of storyboard frames add to a single sketch?', 'A representation of how the interaction progresses over time', ['A guarantee that the backend works','A population-level estimate of error rates','Automatic support for every possible task branch'], 'A storyboard uses key frames to make important moments and progression visible.','pp. 25, 29–30','Storyboards')
    m('What best describes PICTIVE in the lecture?', 'Collaborative interface construction with sticky notes and plastic overlays', ['A complete production database','A statistical test for comparing means','A pointing-time equation'], 'Movable office supplies let people demonstrate interactions and modify designs during the session.','p. 31','Prototyping methods','concept')
    m('A polished prototype triggers discussion only about tiny visual details, while major workflow problems go unchallenged. What risk is illustrated?', 'Fidelity can narrow feedback and make people reluctant to challenge the design', ['Polish guarantees complete functionality','Only paper prototypes can have visual issues','More detail always produces broader feedback'], 'The lecture warns about reactions in the small, reluctance to change the design and mistaking the prototype for a product.','p. 32','Fidelity risks')
    m('A mock-up shows all major screens but has no working backend. Which coverage is emphasized?', 'Horizontal coverage', ['Vertical depth across every function','A complete evolutionary product','A matched-participants design'], 'A horizontal prototype represents broad surface functionality without deep underlying implementation.','p. 33','Prototype coverage')
    m('Only checkout is implemented in depth while most other functions are absent. Which prototype fits?', 'Vertical prototype', ['Purely horizontal prototype','A questionnaire','A persona'], 'A vertical prototype explores a few selected features in depth.','p. 33','Prototype coverage')
    m('A prototype supports only one scripted path and fails if users deviate. What kind of limitation is this?', 'Scenario-based fixed-path coverage', ['Complete task coverage','A field trial with unrestricted use','Evidence that all error paths work'], 'Scenario prototypes and scripted simulations support selected fixed uses rather than unrestricted exploration.','pp. 33, 36','Prototype coverage')
    m('A prototype is discarded after collecting design feedback. Which strategy is this?', 'Throw-away prototyping', ['Evolutionary prototyping','Incremental integration','Selective coding'], 'A throw-away prototype exists to inform design, rather than becoming the final implementation.','p. 34','Prototype lifecycle')
    m('A prototype is repeatedly refined until it becomes the final product. Which strategy is this?', 'Evolutionary prototyping', ['Throw-away prototyping','A fixed storyboard by definition','Between-subjects testing'], 'Evolutionary prototyping develops the prototype itself into the product; incremental development adds separately developed modules.','p. 34','Prototype lifecycle')
    m('What is a trade-off of interface-builder tools?', 'They offer real widgets but can constrain thinking to standard controls', ['They automatically implement every backend function','They cannot represent any interface controls','They make user evaluation unnecessary'], 'Builders support concrete controls and broad mock-ups, while potentially limiting novel or rich graphical interaction.','pp. 41–42','Prototyping tools')
    m('A human interprets requests and generates responses behind a mock interface. Which technique is this?', 'Wizard of Oz', ['A Latin square','Axial coding','An interval scale'], 'A human wizard simulates system responses, making unimplemented or complex functionality testable.','pp. 43–45','Wizard of Oz')
    m('Why does successful Wizard-of-Oz interaction not prove the proposed AI is technically feasible?', 'The observed responses may have been supplied by the human wizard', ['The prototype necessarily used a fully working AI','Users cannot react to simulated behaviour','Human simulation removes every design assumption'], 'The technique tests an interaction concept without demonstrating that an automated implementation can deliver the same responses.','pp. 43–45','Wizard of Oz')
    m('A crucial feature is accessible only through an undisclosed swipe. Which concern is most direct?', 'Visibility of available actions', ['The ratio scale of the task time','The number of grounded-theory codes','The choice of participant matching variable'], 'Gestures can act as hidden commands; a usable design needs discoverable routes to important functions.','pp. 48–49','Design principles')
    m('Save has the same label and behaviour on every screen of one app. Which consistency is illustrated?', 'Internal consistency', ['External consistency only','Random assignment','Cognitive tracing'], 'Internal consistency applies across operations within an application; external consistency concerns other products/devices.','pp. 52–54','Consistency')
    m('Which redesign best supports simplicity without losing required functionality?', 'Remove redundant decoration and group essential controls with consistent spacing', ['Hide every label, including unfamiliar controls','Delete a required confirmation solely to reduce the element count','Give every control a different colour and font'], 'Simplicity removes inessential complexity while retaining the information and controls needed for the task.','pp. 56–57, 61–65','Simplicity')
    m('What is a good layout response to crowded, unrelated controls?', 'Group related items with whitespace and alignment, and separate unrelated groups', ['Draw a heavy border around every item regardless of its role','Remove all hierarchy','Use equal emphasis for every element'], 'Grouping, hierarchy, whitespace, alignment and grids can clarify structure without extra clutter.','pp. 61–65','Visual design')
    m('Why limit unnecessary colour and typography variations?', 'A consistent background pattern lets meaningful differences stand out', ['Every interface must use exactly one colour','Colour always replaces text successfully','Pre-attentive processing guarantees users notice every warning'], 'Excessive variation competes for attention; selective distinction can make relevant information easier to find.','pp. 66–69','Visual design')
    m('Which typography choice best follows the lecture?', 'Use familiar, readable type for long text and reserve unusual styles for short salient uses', ['Use decorative display text for every paragraph','Assume every sans-serif font is equally legible','Choose type only by the newest fashion'], 'The lecture distinguishes long reading from short branding/salient text; actual readability still depends on presentation.','pp. 70–72','Typography')
    m('What makes participatory design different from merely testing users at the end?', 'Representative end users actively collaborate in design decisions throughout iteration', ['Managers always substitute for actual users','Users must create every design from scratch','The first proposed design cannot be revised'], 'Users are first-class collaborators with contextual expertise, rather than only passive test participants.','pp. 75–80','Participatory design')
    m('Experts inspect an interface using Nielsen’s ten heuristics. What is this method?', 'Heuristic evaluation', ['An empirical usability test by definition','A SUS questionnaire','A matched-pairs experiment'], 'Heuristic evaluation is an analytical inspection guided by rules of thumb; it does not require recruited users performing the tasks.','pp. 81–88','Heuristic evaluation')
    m('After a failed payment, a message identifies the problem and explains how to correct it. Which heuristic fits best?', 'Help users recognize, diagnose and recover from errors', ['Aesthetic and minimalist design only','Flexibility and efficiency of use only','Match between system and real world only'], 'The message explains an error and a recovery action; this differs from preventing the invalid action before it occurs.','p. 85','Nielsen heuristics')
    m('A frequent user can use keyboard shortcuts while novices can still use menus. Which heuristic fits?', 'Flexibility and efficiency of use', ['Help and documentation only','Error diagnosis only','System status only'], 'Accelerators can support experienced users without forcing novices to memorize commands.','p. 85','Nielsen heuristics')
    m('A design lets users undo an unintended action and leave an unwanted dialog. Which heuristic is most direct?', 'User control and freedom', ['A requirement for no feedback','A ban on all constraints','The use of a nominal scale'], 'Undo and escape support control and freedom when users take an unwanted route.','p. 85','Nielsen heuristics')
    m('What does the lecture’s evaluator-number graph suggest?', 'Additional evaluators find more problems, with diminishing returns', ['One evaluator finds every important problem','Each additional evaluator always adds exactly the same number of findings','Expert inspection proves that no user testing is needed'], 'The curve rises but flattens; it does not promise universal detection rates or eliminate evaluator bias.','pp. 87–88','Heuristic evaluation')
    m('What is the main focus of a cognitive walkthrough?', 'Whether a new user can discover the action sequence and understand its results', ['Maximum speed after years of expert practice','The production database’s storage capacity','Which statistical test has the largest sample'], 'Walkthroughs assess exploratory learning for specified users and tasks, not primarily highly practised performance.','pp. 81, 89–90','Cognitive walkthrough')
    m('In a walkthrough, the needed button is visible but its label does not suggest the desired outcome. Which question is most directly at risk?', 'Will the user know the correct action is the right one?', ['Will the user notice that the correct action is available? only','What is the standard deviation?','Which component will become the final product?'], 'Noticing a control is different from understanding that it leads to the desired effect.','p. 90','Walkthrough questions')
    m('A new user clicks Save but cannot tell whether the item was stored. Which walkthrough question fails?', 'Will the user understand the feedback?', ['Will the user try to produce the intended effect? only','Is the prototype horizontal?','Was the user randomly assigned?'], 'The breakdown concerns interpreting the system’s response after the action.','p. 90','Walkthrough questions')
    m('What do the letters in GOMS represent?', 'Goals, Operators, Methods and Selection rules', ['Graphics, Objects, Menus and Screens','Groups, Outcomes, Means and Samples','Goals, Observations, Medians and Scales'], 'GOMS separates intended goals, elementary actions, procedures and rules for choosing among procedures.','p. 93','GOMS','concept')
    m('In GOMS, choosing between selecting a word then pressing Delete and repeated Backspace is described by:', 'Selection rules for alternative methods', ['A SUS conversion rule','A colour-contrast ratio','A task-success percentage'], 'Methods are action procedures; selection rules determine which method is used when alternatives exist.','p. 93','GOMS')
    m('For this practice sequence, count 3P + 3K + 1M + 1R. Given P=1.1, K=0.2, M=1.35 and R=0.02 seconds, what is the KLM total?', '5.27 seconds', ['2.67 seconds','4.52 seconds','6.37 seconds'], 'T = 3(1.1) + 3(0.2) + 1.35 + 0.02 = 5.27 seconds. These are supplied practice constants, not a timing table in the lecture.','p. 94','KLM calculation')
    m('Using T = k log2(D/W + 1), with D=300 px, W=100 px and k=0.2 seconds/bit, what is the predicted pointing time?', '0.4 seconds', ['0.6 seconds','0.2 seconds','4 seconds'], 'D/W + 1 = 4; log2(4) = 2; T = 0.2 × 2 = 0.4 seconds. This predicts pointing, not the entire task.','p. 95','Fitts calculation')
    m('What does the lecture’s Fitts model predict if both target distance and width double, with k unchanged?', 'The same pointing time because D/W is unchanged', ['Twice the pointing time','Half the pointing time','Zero pointing time'], 'In this model difficulty depends on the distance-to-width ratio; increasing both equally preserves that ratio.','p. 95','Fitts implications')

    s('Define a conceptual model and distinguish it from visual styling.', 'A conceptual model explains the system’s concepts, operations and relationships at a high level.', 'Visual styling concerns their presentation, such as layout, colours and typography.', 'pp. 3–5, 60','Conceptual models',['High-level organization','Presentation is different'])
    s('Why can a familiar metaphor help without being copied literally?', 'A metaphor transfers familiar knowledge to help users understand an unfamiliar interaction.', 'Keep useful correspondences, but avoid copying limitations or behaviours that conflict with the task.', 'pp. 6–7, 58','Metaphors',['Knowledge transfer','Limits of the analogy'])
    s('Name the four interaction types listed in the lecture.', 'Instructing issues commands; conversing uses a conversational exchange.', 'Manipulating acts on objects; exploring moves through a virtual environment or physical space.', 'p. 8','Interaction types',['Instructing and conversing','Manipulating and exploring'])
    s('State the three defining characteristics of direct manipulation.', 'Continuously represent relevant objects and actions, and use physical actions instead of complex syntax.', 'Provide rapid, reversible actions with immediate feedback on the object of interest.', 'p. 11','Interaction styles',['Visible representation and physical actions','Reversible actions and feedback'])
    s('Why prototype early instead of implementing the first idea fully?', 'A prototype lets the team explore alternatives and obtain concrete user reactions at low cost.', 'Its low investment makes unsuitable ideas easier to discard or revise before implementation.', 'pp. 21–25','Prototyping purpose',['Explore and obtain reactions','Cheap revision and disposal'])
    s('Give one benefit and one limitation of a low-fidelity sketch.', 'A rough sketch is quick to change and directs discussion toward high-level concepts.', 'It may not reveal realistic interaction feel, visual detail or system response time.', 'pp. 24–28','Fidelity limits',['Fast conceptual feedback','Limited realism'])
    s('How does a storyboard differ from a single screen sketch?', 'A single sketch shows one appearance or interface state.', 'A storyboard links key frames to show progression through an interaction or scenario.', 'pp. 25, 29–30','Storyboards',['One state','Sequence of key frames'])
    s('Distinguish horizontal, vertical and scenario prototypes.', 'Horizontal prototypes cover a broad interface surface; vertical prototypes explore selected functions in depth.', 'Scenario prototypes support a specific scripted use path, with little or no deviation.', 'pp. 33, 36','Prototype coverage',['Breadth versus depth','Fixed-path coverage'])
    s('Why are fidelity and functional coverage different dimensions?', 'Fidelity concerns how closely the representation resembles the intended interaction or appearance.', 'Coverage concerns which features or task paths it supports, so polished screens can still be shallow.', 'pp. 22–23, 32–33','Prototype dimensions',['Representation realism','Breadth or depth of support'])
    s('Distinguish throw-away, incremental and evolutionary prototyping.', 'Throw-away prototypes inform design and are discarded; incremental work adds tested components to the product.', 'Evolutionary prototyping repeatedly refines the prototype itself until it becomes the product.', 'p. 34','Prototype lifecycle',['Discard versus add components','Prototype becomes product'])
    s('Explain PICTIVE and how users interact with it.', 'PICTIVE builds a collaborative interface using office supplies, sticky notes and plastic overlays.', 'People move the pieces to demonstrate interaction and modify the proposed design during the session.', 'p. 31','Prototyping methods',['Physical collaborative mock-up','Manipulation and revision'])
    s('Explain Wizard of Oz and one limit of its evidence.', 'A human wizard simulates responses behind a real or mock interface for unimplemented functionality.', 'User reactions can inform interaction design, but they do not prove equivalent automated functionality is feasible.', 'pp. 43–45','Wizard of Oz',['Human simulates responses','No automatic feasibility proof'])
    s('Why can polished prototypes make feedback less useful?', 'People may focus on small details, hesitate to change the design or assume the prototype is finished.', 'Explain its limits and invite feedback on the workflow and alternatives, not just its appearance.', 'p. 32','Fidelity risks',['Polish changes reactions','Clarify and invite broader critique'])
    s('Distinguish internal from external consistency with examples.', 'Internal consistency keeps similar operations alike within an app, such as the same Save behaviour.', 'External consistency follows relevant conventions across apps or devices, such as familiar editing shortcuts.', 'pp. 52–54','Consistency',['Within the application','Across related products'])
    s('Explain simplicity without equating it with hiding every control.', 'Simplicity removes inessential elements and uses regular patterns to reduce processing effort.', 'Retain the labels, controls and feedback needed to make the task understandable and complete.', 'pp. 56–57','Simplicity',['Remove unnecessary complexity','Preserve necessary task support'])
    s('Give two justified visual-design improvements for a crowded form.', 'Group related fields with whitespace and alignment so their relationships are easier to scan.', 'Use clear hierarchy, readable type and selective colour contrast to emphasize important actions.', 'pp. 60–72','Visual design',['Grouping and alignment','Legibility and selective emphasis'])
    s('What makes participatory design more than an end-of-project user test?', 'Representative end users actively collaborate throughout design as experts in their work context.', 'Their feedback informs repeated revisions; they are not expected to be expert interface designers.', 'pp. 75–80','Participatory design',['Active ongoing collaboration','Context expertise and iteration'])
    s('Distinguish heuristic evaluation from a cognitive walkthrough.', 'Heuristic evaluation inspects a design against usability rules across a broad range of use.', 'A cognitive walkthrough follows a task step by step to assess a new user’s exploratory learning.', 'pp. 81, 85, 89–90','Analytical evaluation',['Rule-based broad inspection','New-user task sequence'])
    s('List the four cognitive-walkthrough questions in order.', 'Will users try to produce the effect, and notice that the correct action is available?', 'Will they know the action is right, and understand the feedback after performing it?', 'p. 90','Walkthrough questions',['Goal and action availability','Action meaning and feedback'])
    s('A user notices Submit but cannot tell whether it completes the task. Diagnose the walkthrough issue.', 'The user notices the action but cannot connect its meaning to the intended effect: question 3.', 'Use a clear action label and supporting context that communicate what submission will accomplish.', 'p. 90','Walkthrough application',['Q3 action-to-goal relationship','Targeted label or context fix'])
    s('Explain GOMS using a text-deletion task.', 'Goal: delete a word; operators: pointing, selection and keystrokes; method: select the word then delete.', 'Selection rules choose between that method and alternatives such as repeated Backspace.', 'p. 93','GOMS',['Goal, operators and method','Rule for choosing a method'])
    s('Name the six KLM operators listed in the lecture.', 'K: keystroke; P: pointing; H: homing the hands on a device.', 'D: drawing a straight line; M: mental preparation; R: system response.', 'p. 94','KLM operators',['K, P and H','D, M and R'])
    s('Calculate 3P + 3K + M + R using P=1.1, K=0.2, M=1.35 and R=0.02 seconds.', 'T = 3(1.1) + 3(0.2) + 1.35 + 0.02.', 'T = 5.27 seconds for exactly the stated operators and supplied practice timings.', 'p. 94','KLM calculation',['Correct operator counts','Correct sum and assumptions'])
    s('State the lecture’s Fitts formula and its design implication.', 'T = k log2(D/W + 1), with D as target distance and W as width along the movement direction.', 'Larger targets or shorter distances reduce predicted pointing time when other terms remain fixed.', 'p. 95','Fitts law',['Formula and variable meaning','Distance/width implication'])
    assert sum(q['type']=='mcq' for q in bank)==40
    assert sum(q['type']=='short' for q in bank)==24
    assert all(len(q['answer'])==2 and len(' '.join(q['answer']).split())<=40 for q in bank if q['type']=='short')
    return bank
